      "data": {
        "text": "**Choosing OCR Provider**\n\nI'm currently focused on selecting a concrete OCR and layout extraction provider for the `extract_layout.py` script. Google's suite seems promising as a potential option, and I will be considering its features. I'm aiming to make the provider easily identifiable in the code.\n\n\n**Defining the Function**\n\nI've decided to implement the `extract_layout_from_document` function within `extract_layout.py`. My plan involves utilizing the `google-cloud-vision` library, specifically the `vision.ImageAnnotatorClient`. The function will take image bytes as input, leverage the `DOCUMENT_TEXT_DETECTION` feature of the Vision API to extract layout elements and return the extracted layout data.\n\n\n**Refining Google Vision Plan**\n\nI've decided on the `DOCUMENT_TEXT_DETECTION` feature from `google-cloud-vision` for now, recognizing its utility in capturing layout elements like blocks and lines directly. While table detection is a consideration, it appears `DOCUMENT_TEXT_DETECTION` provides a suitable foundation for this task. I'm focusing on how to best extract those elements from the API's response, considering pages, blocks, paragraphs, words, and symbols.\n\n\n**Implementing the API Call**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the function's internal structure in `extract_layout.py`. My thinking involves direct use of `google.cloud.vision` for the API interaction. I intend to create and instantiate the `ImageAnnotatorClient` within the function. Then, I plan to construct the image and feature request, ultimately calling `annotate_image` and extracting blocks and lines from the returned `full_text_annotation`. I'm going to start parsing that annotation, for this version of the script.\n\n\n**Finalizing the Implementation**\n\nI'm now solidifying the code within `extract_layout.py`. My focus is on making the function self-contained with clear identification of Google Cloud Vision, and making sure I parse the `full_text_annotation` to get blocks and lines. I'm choosing to return a list of dictionaries. The dictionaries will represent extracted blocks and lines along with their bounding box data. This should make the returned layout data easy to use.\n\n\n**Implementing the Code**\n\nI'm now writing the Python code, focusing on clarity and ease of use. I've decided to import the `google.cloud.vision` library and instantiate the `ImageAnnotatorClient` within the `extract_layout_from_document` function. My output will be a list of dictionaries. The dictionaries will represent extracted blocks and lines along with their bounding box data. This should make the returned layout data easy to use.\n\n\n",
        "time": {
          "end": 1789860615092,
          "start": 1789860607217
        },
